// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

Why go to the trouble of using a formal signal carrier directory, or "medium"?

The SimpleSal development model has the user create an instance of a managed blob, from the repo,
through a medium.  All managed objects are transported through the medium to and from the repo.
Changes made to the instance are pushed out to the repo through the medium, under source control.

The contents of the medium directory matches the contents of the development directory.  The
contents of the medium directory matches the repo contents, except the repo metadata is not present.
The term source control refers to managing the differences between the directories, over time.

Each instance or medium will contain a read-only or writable copy of a repository's managed blob.
The term "read-only" means "something blocks any modification of the file".
The term "writable" means "nothing blocks any ...".

The repository directory is the possession of the manager and can be changed at any moment.  Modifying
the content of repository directory outside the manager application is only for the most advanced user.

The repo manager uses the file system to enforce the "something/nothing blocks" modification controls.
Medium and development modification controls are implemented by the user, personally, in their head.

A Medium directory with a copy of a repository's development tree can be referred to directly by a
build path directive.  In the Arduino ecosystem, each App directory contains all the files included;
each App's directory will contain a directory tree that matches the Medium and the repository.

A Medium directory with a writable copy can also be referred to directly in a build path directive.
This makes the Medium directory into a development directory, and makes the development directory
into the Medium. This kind of dilemma is a good reason to strictly partition instance/medium/repo.

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
